[
  {"id":"SEC-1","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/shape.js","line":17,"what_breaks":"A stored order date is only checked to be a string, so a hand-edited document with an unparseable date passes as valid. The order screens then crash instead of showing the R-20 blocked screen. No copy way out is offered, Reload repeats the crash, and only Erase all data on the customer list stays reachable.","requirement":"R-20","rule":"JS-08","evidence":"Input under invoice-app: {\"version\":1,\"nextOrderNumber\":2,\"customers\":[{\"id\":\"c1\",\"name\":\"A\",\"email\":\"\",\"phone\":\"\",\"address\":\"\"}],\"orders\":[{\"id\":\"o1\",\"number\":\"ORD-0001\",\"customerId\":\"c1\",\"date\":\"not-a-date\",\"notes\":\"\",\"lines\":[]}]}. isOrder (shape.js:17) sees a string, so load() returns ok:true and canWrite is true. format.js:14 builds new Date('not-a-dateT00:00:00Z'), an Invalid Date, and Intl.DateTimeFormat.format throws RangeError. Callers are order-list.js:51 (#/orders), customer-detail.js:69 (#/customers/c1) and order-view.js:30 (#/orders/o1). render() throws and the window error listener shows only APP-01 with Reload. The data model sketch types the field as YYYY-MM-DD, and shape.js checks number with a regex but not date. No test covers a malformed date string: storage.spec.mjs:204 only tries the number 5. Not reachable through the forms, because isRealDate guards them.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-2","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/shape.js","line":12,"what_breaks":"Stored quantity and unitPriceCents are only checked to be safe integers. A valid-looking document can make the line total unsafe, which throws on the order list, on that customer's detail and on the order view. The order and the customer then cannot be removed through their screens, the lock-out ADR-004 says the shape check must avoid; only Erase all data remains. Negative values also pass and show broken text.","requirement":"R-20","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Input: a line with \"quantity\":9007199254740991,\"unitPriceCents\":2. isCount passes both and load() returns ok:true. lineTotalCents gives about 1.8e16, requireSafeInteger throws (money.js:25), reached through orderTotalCents from toRow (orders.js:26) on #/orders, the search and the customer detail, and from order-view.js:91 and :171. The order view cannot render, so the line and the order cannot be removed through their screens, and the customer stays blocked by R-04. Only APP-01 appears, with no copy option. Separately, \"unitPriceCents\":-50 passes and priceInputText(-50) gives -1.-50 in the edit field (Math.floor gives -1, -50 % 100 gives -50). The accepted test at storage.spec.mjs:328 only uses 10000. Not reachable through the forms.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-3","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/shape.js","line":53,"what_breaks":"nextOrderNumber accepts any safe integer, including negative values and the maximum. Creating one order then writes a document the app itself refuses on the next load, so a readable data set becomes blocked as corrupt.","requirement":"R-06, R-20","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Input 1: \"nextOrderNumber\":-5 with no orders. orderNumber gives String(-5).padStart(4,'0'), so ORD-00-5, and save succeeds. On reload ORDER_NUMBER (/^ORD-(\\d+)$/) fails, isOrder is false and load returns corrupt. Input 2: \"nextOrderNumber\":9007199254740991. createOrder (orders.js:44) stores 9007199254740992, isCount is false on reload, and load returns corrupt. ADR-004 says the counter starts at 1. Not reachable from a document the app wrote itself.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-4","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/shape.js","line":42,"what_breaks":"Order number uniqueness compares strings, so two orders with the same numeric number pass the shape check and R-06 uniqueness is not enforced on stored data.","requirement":"R-06","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Input: two orders with \"number\":\"ORD-1\" and \"number\":\"ORD-0001\" and \"nextOrderNumber\":2. allUnique sees two different strings, and orderNumberValue gives 1 for both, which is below 2, so the document is accepted and two orders show the same numeric sequence. Hand-edited data only.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-5","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/storage.js","line":22,"what_breaks":"Extra keys in a stored document are neither refused nor removed. They reach the domain, are written back on every save, and customer-level extras reach the R-29 export. ADR-004 fixes the document to the fields of the sketch with nothing added.","requirement":"R-29","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Input: a valid document plus \"backup\":[{\"name\":\"Old customer\",\"email\":\"old@example.com\"}] at the root, or \"nationalId\":\"123\" on a customer. hasValidShape checks only the named fields. Rest destructuring at storage.js:22 keeps backup, every command spreads ...data, and save writes it back (storage.js:50), so deleting all orders and the customer leaves that personal data stored despite the R-04 text. export.js:11 clones the customer record whole, so nationalId lands in the export file. updateCustomer (customers.js:29) silently drops the same extra field. Hand-edited data only. No test feeds an extra key.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-6","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/shape.js","line":8,"what_breaks":"Stored ids are only checked to be strings, and routeToHash puts them into the hash without encoding. Some records then cannot be opened, or open the wrong screen. The value stays inside the fragment, so it cannot become a script URL.","requirement":"R-28","rule":"SE-01","evidence":"Input: customer \"id\":\"\" gives href #/customers/, and parseRoute (router.js:41) refuses an empty part, so not-found. \"id\":\"new\" gives #/customers/new, which opens the new customer form instead of the record. \"id\":\"a b\" gives location.hash #/customers/a%20b, parseRoute does not decode it, the find fails, so not-found. Line \"id\":\"\": openForm('', button) in order-view.js:229 treats it as no line and opens Add instead of Edit. The record cannot be deleted through its screen. Hand-edited data only.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-7","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/domain/validation.js","line":33,"what_breaks":"Text made only of invisible characters passes the required-field checks, because trim() does not remove them. A customer or line can be saved with no visible name or description. Direction override characters can also make one name look like another, despite the A-12 goal of telling customers apart.","requirement":"R-01, R-11","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Input: paste U+200B (zero width space) as the name. U+200B is not ECMAScript whitespace, so textOf keeps it, length is 1, lengthError passes, and createCustomer stores it. The list row at customer-list.js:32 has a link with no visible text. The same path applies to the line description (validation.js:105, 109). Literally this meets R-01 (1 to 100 characters after trimming), so it is advisory. Whether invisible-only text counts as empty is a decision for the human. No test covers it.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-8","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/storage.js","line":50,"what_breaks":"save() has no check of its own, so a malformed value overwrites good data. The write lock only guards the load state. The spread also runs after version, so a version key in data replaces the constant. Validation of the document lives only in the callers.","requirement":"R-20","rule":"SE-02","evidence":"Direct call: save({}) writes {\"version\":1} over the stored document, and the next load is corrupt. save({version:2,...}) writes version 2 because ...data comes after version: VERSION, and the next load is newer. No reachable caller found: commit() receives only result.data from a successful domain command, and parseDocument strips version. Probe that settles it: a storage test that calls save after a good load with a value lacking customers, and one with a version key.","proven":false},
  {"id":"SEC-9","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/storage.js","line":52,"what_breaks":"Quota detection depends on the exact error name. A browser that reports quota under another name gets STO-02 instead of STO-01, so the message differs while the failure stays visible and the data stays safe.","requirement":"R-21","rule":"JS-07","evidence":"Only name === 'QuotaExceededError' maps to quota. Older Firefox used NS_ERROR_DOM_QUOTA_REACHED, which I could not confirm for current versions. Tests use only a fake DOMException with the standard name. Probe that settles it: the manual R-21 check of S11, run in current Firefox and Safari, confirming STO-01 appears.","proven":false},
  {"id":"SEC-10","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":".github/workflows/gate.yml","line":15,"what_breaks":"CI actions are referenced by a moving major tag, so the code that runs in the pipeline can change without a commit here. The kit checkout is pinned by SHA, but the actions themselves are not.","requirement":"none","rule":"DR-04","evidence":"gate.yml:15 and :20 use actions/checkout@v7, gate.yml:27 uses actions/setup-node@v7, and node-version is 22 with no exact version. DR-04 requires pinning by commit SHA, digest or exact version. The impact is limited because permissions are contents: read and persist-credentials is false.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-11","class":"contract","severity":"advisory","file":"src/domain/validation.js","line":7,"what_breaks":"The quantity rule differs from ADR-007, which says ^\\d+$ then the range. Leading zeros that give a valid value are refused at six digits or more, but accepted at five or fewer.","requirement":"R-11","evidence":"Input 000001: QUANTITY_PATTERN /^\\d{1,5}$/ fails, so the app shows the quantity error, though the value is 1. Input 00001 is accepted as 1. This is the only effect. Very long digit strings are refused either way, and no invalid value is stored.","proven":true},
  {"id":"SEC-12","class":"security","severity":"advisory","file":"src/storage/storage.js","line":15,"what_breaks":"parseDocument rethrows any JSON.parse error that is not a SyntaxError, so load() can throw instead of returning a result. start() calls load() before it registers the error listeners, so such a throw leaves an empty page with no message. Writing stays locked, so no data is lost, but the failure is not visible.","requirement":"R-20","rule":"EO-01","evidence":"storage.js:15 rethrows, and createStorage.load has no try around parseDocument (storage.js:42). main.js:189 calls storage.load() while the error and unhandledrejection listeners are only added at main.js:197 and :198. No trigger found: it needs an engine error from JSON.parse, such as an out-of-memory error on a very large stored text. Probe that settles it: a storage test with a backing store whose text makes JSON.parse throw a non-SyntaxError, or a manual check in each target browser with a stored document near the quota.","proven":false},
